\title[Gaussian binomials, subgroup counts and class fields]{Gaussian Binomial Coefficients over an Arbitrary Base, Subgroup Counts of Finite Groups, and the Intermediate Fields of a Hilbert Class Field}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{19 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
Let $K$ be a number field and $H$ an unramified abelian extension whose Galois group is identified
with the ideal class group $\Cl(K)$. By the Galois correspondence the intermediate fields of $H/K$
correspond to the subgroups of $\Cl(K)$, so the arithmetic question ``how many intermediate
fields?'' becomes the group-theoretic question ``how many subgroups?''. We develop both halves of
the answer.

Arithmetically we prove, entirely within the integers and for \emph{every} base $q \ge 2$ --- not
merely for prime powers realised by a finite field --- that the Gaussian binomial coefficient
$\binom{n}{k}_q = \prod_{i<k}(q^{n}-q^{i}) \big/ \prod_{i<k}(q^{k}-q^{i})$ is an exact division,
satisfies the $q$-Pascal recursion and its dual, is symmetric and strictly positive for $k \le n$,
and obeys the $q$-factorial identity $[k]_q!\,[n-k]_q!\,\binom{n}{k}_q = [n]_q!$ with
$[m]_q! = \prod_{j<m}(q^{j+1}-1)$. Summing rows gives the Galois numbers
$G_q(n) = \sum_{k\le n}\binom{n}{k}_q$, for which we prove the three-term recursion
$G_q(n+2) = 2G_q(n+1) + (q^{n+1}-1)G_q(n)$, with $G_q(2)=q+3$; for $q=2$ this generates
$1,2,5,16,67,374$ and for $q=3$ it gives $G_3(4)=212$.

Group-theoretically we prove that the subgroup lattice of a direct product of two finite groups of
coprime orders is the product of the two lattices --- with no commutativity assumed --- whence the
subgroup count is multiplicative over coprime factors; that a finite cyclic group of order $N$ has
exactly $d(N)$ subgroups; and that a group of order $p^{2}$ and exponent $p$ has $p+1$ subgroups of
order $p$ and $p+3$ in total.

Combining: a Hilbert class field with cyclic class group of order $n$ has $d(n)$ intermediate
fields; with class group $(\Z/p)^{r}$ it has $G_p(r)$, exactly $\binom{r}{k}_p$ of degree $p^{k}$
over $K$; and with class group $(\Z/p)^{r}\times(\Z/q)^{s}$, $p \ne q$, it has $G_p(r)G_q(s)$. In
particular the number of intermediate fields is not a function of the class number: at $h_K=4$ it
is $3$ or $5$, at $h_K=12$ it is $6$ or $10$, and at $h_K=p^{2}$ it is $3$ or $p+3$.
\end{abstract}

